# Baseline Profile 生成文件：标记为文本，统一使用 LF 换行，避免 CRLF 抖动
app/src/release/generated/baselineProfiles/*.txt text eol=lf

# Claude P conformance corpus and specifications.
#
# These files are hashed and vendored byte-for-byte, so their working-tree form must not
# depend on the machine that checked them out. Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` yields
# CRLF for anyone on Windows and LF everywhere else, and a hash computed on one machine
# could never match a hash computed on another.
#
# This is a working-tree guard, NOT the correctness argument. The specification digests
# are canonical (claudep/02-wire-protocol-v1.md §12.11): they normalize CRLF to LF and
# reject a bare CR, so they produce the same value from either working tree. This file
# exists because MANIFEST.sha256 hashes raw bytes and cannot do that, and because a corpus
# file that silently changed shape between checkouts would make the vendoring comparison
# meaningless even where the digests still matched.
#
# If this block were removed the canonical digests would still be correct; the manifest
# would become checkout-dependent. Do not treat it as the only thing holding either up.
claudep/conformance/** text eol=lf
claudep/02-wire-protocol-v1.md text eol=lf
claudep/01-architecture-and-trust-boundaries.md text eol=lf

# Claude P M2 bridge conformance corpus.
#
# Authored in `rikkahub-claude-p-server` and vendored here byte-for-byte for M2-B, which
# implements the Kotlin half of the same byte contract. Same reasoning as the block above:
# `MANIFEST.sha256` hashes raw bytes, so the working-tree form must not depend on the
# machine that checked it out. The server copy is authoritative; this one is verified
# against it and is never regenerated to make a build green.
claudep/conformance-bridge/vectors/** text eol=lf
